WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.680
Up until the 1970s,
cryptography had

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.150
been based on symmetric keys.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.170
That is, the sender
encrypts their message

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:12.980
using a specific
key, and the receiver

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.120
decrypts using an identical key.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.670
As you may recall,
encryption is a mapping

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.010
from some message using a
specific key to a cipher text

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.310
message.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.320
To decrypt a cipher text,
you use the same key

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.890
to reverse the mapping.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.320
So for Alice and Bob to
communicate securely,

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.290
they must first
share identical keys.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.300
However, establishing
a shared key

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.260
is often impossible if Alice
and Bob can't physically

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.650
meet, or requires extra
communications overhead

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.550
when using the
Diffie-Hellman key exchange.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.480
Plus, if Alice
needs to communicate

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.450
with multiple people,
perhaps she's a bank,

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.090
then she's going
to have to exchange

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.200
distinct keys with each person.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.610
Now she'll have to
manage all of these keys

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.990
and send thousands of messages
just to establish them.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.280
Could there be a simpler way?

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.960
In 1970, James Ellis, a British
engineer and mathematician,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.820
was working on an idea
for non-secret encryption.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.800
It's based on a simple
yet clever concept.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.920
Lock and unlock are
inverse operations.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.380
Alice could buy a
lock, keep the key,

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.460
and send the open lock to Bob.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.280
Bob then locks his message
and sends it back to Alice.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.940
No keys are exchanged.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.900
This means she could publish
the lock widely and let

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.025
anyone in the world use
it to send her a message.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:56.760
And she now only needs to
keep track of a single key.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.710
Ellis never arrived at
a mathematical solution,

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:07.810
though he had an intuitive
sense of how it should work.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.630
The idea is based
on splitting a key

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:14.400
into two parts, an encryption
key and a decryption key.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.460
The decryption key performs the
inverse, or undo, operation,

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.690
which was applied by
the encryption key.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.380
Too see how inverse
keys could work,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:28.250
let's do a simplified
example with colors.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.700
How could Bob send
Alice a specific color

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.740
without Eve, who is always
listening, intercepting it?

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.880
The inverse of some
color is called

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:41.880
a complementary color,
which when added to it

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:48.960
produces white, undoing the
effect of the first color.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.550
In this example, we
assume that mixing colors

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.750
is a one-way function because
it's fast to mix colors

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.930
and output a third, and
it's much slower to undo.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.040
Alice first generates
her private key

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.400
by randomly selecting
a color, say, red.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.570
Next, assume Alice uses
a secret color machine

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:14.200
to find the exact
complement of her red.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.856
And nobody else
has access to this.

00:03:16.856 --> 00:03:22.930
This results in cyan, which she
sends to Bob as her public key.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:26.710
Let's say Bob wants to send
a secret yellow to Alice.

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:29.130
He mixes this with
her public color

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:32.540
and sends the resulting
mixture back to Alice.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:37.330
Now Alice adds her private
color to Bob's mixture.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:40.430
This undoes the effect
of her public color,

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.050
leaving her with
Bob's secret color.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.510
Notice Eve has no easy
way to find Bob's yellow,

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.180
since she needs Alice's
private red to do so.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:50.910
This is how it should work.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:52.720
However, a mathematical
solution was

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.510
needed to make this
work in practice.

